  "verbalization": "Chenodeoxycholic acid (Chenodeoxycholic Acid) is implicated in the pathogenesis of choledocholithiasis through a series of interconnected indications that suggest a potential mechanistic link. The evidence indicates that Chenodeoxycholic Acid is associated with cholelithiasis and cholecystolithiasis, both of which are conditions involving the formation of stones in the gallbladder or bile ducts. These conditions are further linked to choledocholithiasis, suggesting a possible pathway where bile acid metabolism is disrupted, leading to stone formation. The presence of a direct indication from Chenodeoxycholic Acid to choledocholithiasis, along with indirect pathways through cholelithiasis and cholecystolithiasis, implies that the drug may influence bile acid composition or secretion, potentially altering the environment in the bile ducts and promoting stone formation. The cumulative scores from the three paths suggest a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, though further mechanistic validation is needed to confirm the exact role of Chenodeoxycholic Acid in the development of choledocholithiasis.",
